WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.205 --> 00:00:02.705
(light music)

00:00:04.192 --> 00:00:06.771
- Ships are magnificent
creatures of the sea,

00:00:06.771 --> 00:00:10.771
built, designed for the
deep waters of the ocean.

00:00:10.771 --> 00:00:13.021
But once you bring these
magnificent creatures

00:00:13.021 --> 00:00:15.291
to inland waters around the world,

00:00:15.291 --> 00:00:17.281
they get fairly clumsy.

00:00:17.281 --> 00:00:19.870
(light music)

00:00:19.870 --> 00:00:23.935
- A ship at sea may travel
3,000 miles in a straight line.

00:00:23.935 --> 00:00:26.441
But the difficulty happens
when you get to port,

00:00:26.441 --> 00:00:28.810
and that's when the ship pilot takes over.

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:30.727
(light music)

00:00:30.727 --> 00:00:34.258
He's essentially like a
valet who parks your car.

00:00:34.258 --> 00:00:36.960
The pilot comes out usually
on a small pilot boat,

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.979
and then they climb up
the side of the ship

00:00:38.979 --> 00:00:42.081
on a small ladder, up to the wheelhouse,

00:00:42.081 --> 00:00:43.829
to get the ship into the port.

00:00:43.829 --> 00:00:47.496
- If it floats, we are
capable of moving it.

00:00:50.719 --> 00:00:53.219
(light music)

00:00:55.508 --> 00:00:57.550
- Here at MPI, we train ship pilots

00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:01.069
to guide the largest moving
objects on the planet.

00:01:01.069 --> 00:01:02.908
We have traditional classroom training.

00:01:02.908 --> 00:01:05.239
We have a state-of-the-art ship simulator,

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:07.739
and then we have manned model.

00:01:09.936 --> 00:01:12.406
(light music)

00:01:12.406 --> 00:01:15.326
Although these models
may look like toy boats,

00:01:15.326 --> 00:01:17.285
they’re actually precise tools

00:01:17.285 --> 00:01:19.830
used to mimic the behavior of vessels.

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:22.330
(light music)

00:01:23.899 --> 00:01:25.223
If you took a full-size vessel

00:01:25.223 --> 00:01:27.001
and shrunk it down 25 times,

00:01:27.001 --> 00:01:30.001
you could get very reliable results.

00:01:31.182 --> 00:01:33.185
- A human being on our models would be

00:01:33.185 --> 00:01:36.575
like an inch and three-quarters tall.

00:01:36.575 --> 00:01:37.732
- The manned models are between

00:01:37.732 --> 00:01:41.434
24,000 and 30,000 pounds of weight,

00:01:41.434 --> 00:01:44.158
and they have 0.4 horsepower.

00:01:44.158 --> 00:01:46.825
They behave exactly like a ship.

00:01:49.388 --> 00:01:50.302
- This is real.

00:01:50.302 --> 00:01:52.657
This is how it's done
onboard all the ships

00:01:52.657 --> 00:01:53.817
around the world.

00:01:53.817 --> 00:01:55.367
Starboard 10 degrees.

00:01:55.367 --> 00:01:56.617
- Starboard 10.

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:00.222
- We surely would not want to practice

00:02:00.222 --> 00:02:02.542
with a perfectly good ship,

00:02:02.542 --> 00:02:04.890
and say, "Captain, we
have to drop anchors,"

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:07.772
and simulate broke down
engines and steering.

00:02:07.772 --> 00:02:10.762
So we do that with these vessels.

00:02:10.762 --> 00:02:13.341
(light music)

00:02:13.341 --> 00:02:15.557
It's a profession that, I think, for me,

00:02:15.557 --> 00:02:19.018
at the top of the maritime field.

00:02:19.018 --> 00:02:23.185
It's an awesome responsibility,
and very challenging.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.243
When I finish the walk down a dock,

00:02:26.243 --> 00:02:27.688
look back at the ship and say,

00:02:27.688 --> 00:02:30.438
"Yes, that was a good challenge."

00:02:31.457 --> 00:02:33.957
(light music)

